    {
      "title": "Your Success Depends on This",
      "slug": "your-success-depends-on-this",
      "displayOrder": 20,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 101,
        "lessonOrder": 20
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<strong>You are making progress!</strong> ... we've spent a lot of time planning, practicing and thinking about knitting ... are you ready to do some \"real knitting\"?<br><strong>DON'T SKIP THIS SECTION!</strong> <br>Your success with this course AND your success with your knitting machine depends on your understanding and mastering the art of knitting gauge swatches.<br>Without a swatch we are knitting in the dark.\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>How is the finished shawl going to feel in the yarn and stitch pattern you've chosen?</li>\r\n<li>Do you have enough yarn?</li>\r\n<li>Do you have enough needles on your machine for the size you chose?</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n\r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>SWATCHING IS NOT OPTIONAL</strong></p>\r\n<img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"https://www.knititnow.com/images/blog_images/2017/swatching_is_not_optional.jpg\" /></div>\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 1409,
              "order": 1
            }
          ],
          "title": "Don't skip this section",
          "slug": "don-t-skip-this-section",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 444,
            "blockType": "Video"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<div class=\"lead\"><b>If you haven't used your chosen yarn before, how do you know the best tension to knit your shawlette? </div></b>\r\n\r\nKnitters tend to use the terms \"tension swatch\" and \"gauge swatch\" interchangeably, lets take a look at both.",
              "legacyComponentId": 1410,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<div class=\"cleanwell\">\r\n\r\n<h4><strong>GAUGE SWATCH</strong></h4>\r\nThe <strong>gauge swatch</strong> is the \"official\" swatch used when designing a garment.\r\n<br>\r\nIt is knit at a specific size, marked for accurate stitch and row measurement and \"dressed\" as the final garment will be treated.<br>\r\n<blockquote class=\"smallblock\">\r\n<em>TIP: Whatever method you use to create a swatch, the key is to correctly measure it and calculate your gauge.</em></blockquote>\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/16/swatch_measure.jpg\" />\r\n</div>",
              "legacyComponentId": 1411,
              "order": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<div class=\"cleanwell\">\r\n\r\n<h4><strong>TENSION SWATCH</strong></h4>\r\n<em>When I pick up a new cone of yarn, I can't wait to thread up my machine and see what the yarn looks like.   From the initial cast on, I start guessing what setting on my tension dial I need to use.  I knit a few inches and reach down and start to feel the resulting fabric. <br>Once I get a feel for the yarn (what machine, general tension setting at stockinette, etc) I create a<strong> tension swatch</strong>.  This helps me fine-tune the tension needed to achieve the fabric I like and allows me to play with various techniques.  <strong>I'm often very surprised that a yarn looks great in tuck, and awful in slip</strong>, for example.  Adding a few eyelets here and there tells me if the yarn will work up in lace.</em><br>Let's practice knitting a <strong>tension swatch.</strong>\r\n</div>",
              "legacyComponentId": 1412,
              "order": 5
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "\r\n\r\n<ol class=\"instruction-list\">\r\n<li>Play!</li>\r\n<li>Start knitting stockenette at the tightest tension that will knit.  (If the tension is too tight, you'll see stitches that don't form properly.</li>\r\n<li>Knit 2-4 inches. Knit a marker row (change colors, work a few eyelets across the row or knit a sewing thread in with the main yarn)</li>\r\n<li>Keep knitting sections, marking the tension dial number in each section.  Knit the last section as loosely as possiblle, even if you think this will be too loose ... I'm often very pleasantly surprised at the result.</li>\r\n<li>Transfer every other needle to out of work postion and knit a section - put all needles back into work.</li>\r\n<li>Work a simple tuck, or slip, or any stitch pattern of your choice ... base your tension dial setting on the stockinette sections you just knit.</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n<blockquote class=\"smallblock\">The point of this exercise is to put the yarn through it's paces ... ignite your creativity, and get a feel for what the yarn can do.  If you don't have a huge cone, you can always re-wind this knitting. At this point, you probably know exactly what you want to do with this yarn.  Knit your Gauge Swatch, dress it and and you are ready to design and knit your sweater.</blockquote>\r\n\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/16/tension_swatch.jpg\" />\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 1413,
              "order": 7
            }
          ],
          "title": "Step 1: Gauge swatch verses Tension Swatch",
          "slug": "step-1-gauge-swatch-verses-tension-swatch",
          "order": 3,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 445,
            "blockType": "Practice"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "Hand knitters use the excuse \"It takes too much time to knit a swatch\", then they are often disappointed that their knitting doesn't fit.<br>\r\n<b>Machine knitters, rejoice!</b>  Knitting a swatch is fast and the information it supplies is invaluable ...even for something as simple as a blanket or shawl.",
              "legacyComponentId": 1414,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "178357273",
              "title": null,
              "legacyComponentId": 1415,
              "order": 2
            }
          ],
          "title": "Step 2: Knitting swatches",
          "slug": "step-2-knitting-swatches",
          "order": 4,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 446,
            "blockType": "Video"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "\r\n\r\n<div class=\"lead\">Time to get knitting!</div>\r\n<ol class=\"Instruction-list\">\r\n<li>Knit a swatch using any method.</li>\r\n<li>Let it rest</li>\r\n<li>Dress the swatch (treat it as you will treat your blanket)</li>\r\n<li>Accurately measure YOUR stitch and row gauge</li>\r\n<li>Record your gauge below</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/16/2hr/swatch_example.jpg\" /><br />Does your swatch look similar to this one?\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 1416,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<blockquote class=\"smallblock\"><em><b>TIP:</b> attach a string tag to your swatch recording all the details ... it's funny how easy it is to forget. Include yarn name, machine, tension, measurements, and stitch pattern number (if appropriate)</em></blockquote>",
              "legacyComponentId": 1419,
              "order": 4
            }
          ],
          "title": "Step 3: Your turn ... Get Knitting",
          "slug": "step-3-your-turn-get-knitting",
          "order": 5,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 447,
            "blockType": "Practice"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<div class=\"lead\">Estimate the amount of yarn needed</div>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div class=\"col-12-xs\">\r\nBecause of the odd shape of this pattern, the best way to estimate the amount of yarn needed is by stitches instead of dimensions.<br><b>What do you need to do? </b><small>(We'll do the math for you)</small><br><ol class=\"instruction-list\">\r\n<li>Record the number of stitches in your swatch (<b>the entire swatch, not just the center</b>)</li>\r\n<li>Record the number of rows in your swatch (<b>the entire swatch from cast on edge to bind off edge</b>)</li>\r\n<li>Weigh your swatch in grams</li>\r\n</ol></div>\r\n<div class=\"col-12-xs\"><img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/16/swirl_shawl/yarn_estimate_swatch.png\"></div>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<blockquote class=\"blockquote_style\">\r\nNOTE: This estimate is optional.  If you don't have the information (or you are just \"winging it\") The pattern is written so you know the current depth of the scarf/shawl as you are following the row by row instructions.<br>\r\nIf you are running short of yarn, you'll know your longest edge.<br>ALSO, using this tool will help you determine if you have enough yarn BEFORE you start knitting.\r\n</blockquote>\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 1421,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
