        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "Unlike hand knitters, we can't measure our work as we are knitting.  The stitches are stretched and distorted as they are formed on the needlebed.<br>\r\nThis is why swatching and accurate gauge measurement are critical.<br>\r\nWe keep track of stitches AND rows to ensure that our finished piece is the correct size.<br>When knitting this Shawlette, it is tempting to \"guestimate\" the length while the knitting is still on the machine.<br>In the first image below, my stitches are spread over almost 20 inches.\r\n<br>\r\nIn the second image, I've removed the work from the needles and you can see that it is considerably narrower.  Letting it rest will also change the size.<br>\r\n\r\n<h4>Lesson Learned:  Don't measure your knitting while it is on the machine.</h4>Rely on your gauge and the math in your pattern to create the correct size knitted piece.<br>\r\n<img class=\"img-thumbnail\"src=\"/challenge/images/swirl_shawl/dont_measure.jpg\">\r\n\r\n<img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/16/swirl_shawl/dont_measure1.jpg\">\r\n\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 1448,
              "order": 1
            }
          ],
          "title": "Don't measure on the machine!",
          "slug": "don-t-measure-on-the-machine",
          "order": 6,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 457,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        },
